WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.260
The Sun,

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:05.120
smooth and round and peaceful.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.040
Except when it suddenly vomits radiation
and plasma in random directions.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.580
These solar flares and coronal
mass ejections, or CMEs,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.340
can hit Earth and have serious
consequences for humanity.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.540
How exactly do they work?

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.140
How bad could they be?

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.020
And can we prepare for them?

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.500
[Intro music]

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.880
While the Sun seems pretty solid,
it's actually like a very hot ocean.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:44.380
So hot that it rips atoms into electrons and nuclei,
all flowing around each other in a plasma.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.100
This plasma is pushed around and
shaped by the Sun's magnetic field.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.520
Similar to how the Sun's gravitational field reaches
out to the planets and shapes their orbits.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.760
But magnetism is very different from gravity.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.960
Magnetism is one part of a dual force: 
Electromagnetism.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.940
Electricity creates magnetic fields,
and magnetic fields create electricity.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.240
On the Sun, the plasma—made of electrically
charged protons and electrons—

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.700
creates a magnetic field as they move,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.980
and this magnetic field then
shapes the flow of particles.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.180
They're stuck in a dynamic
feedback loop called a dynamo,

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.620
which keeps the sun's
magnetic field alive.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.960
This magnetic field stores
enormous amounts of energy

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.120
and leaks out over the Solar System.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.820
It carries with it a constant trickle of solar plasma,
like a light rain, known as the solar wind,

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.720
creating a sort of space weather.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.660
But it isn't always calm and smooth.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.900
As the sun's plasma churns
and flows around itself,

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.860
its magnetic field gets
all kinked and twisted.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.100
This creates magnetic knots that
build up enormous amounts of energy.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.600
When the magnetic knots break—like a
tangle of springs exploding outwards—

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.720
the Sun can vomit plasma and other
awful things into the Solar System.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.920
These solar storms come in many types,

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.540
like solar flares; a tidal wave
of high-energy radiation.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.840
They race through the solar system at the speed
of light, sweeping up protons in the solar wind,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.520
accelerating them into a
high-speed solar-proton storm.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.660
Then, there are coronal mass ejections,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.940
which rip millions or billions of tons
of plasma from the Sun's atmosphere,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:29.120
catapulting it through the solar system at
speeds of up to 9 million km/h.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.300
When these monsters hit us,
nothing happens on Earth.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.420
While even smaller storms
can damage satellites,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.020
affect radio communication,

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.740
or be dangerous to astronauts,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.540
for people on the surface,
space weather is harmless.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.660
Earth's atmosphere protects us from
the worst effects of a solar flare

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.880
by absorbing the blast of X-rays
high up in the atmosphere,

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.920
well before it reaches the surface.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.200
The electrified plasma from a CME is
deflected by the Earth's magnetic field,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.340
diverting the energy storm to
the North and South Poles,

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.940
where energetic particles
fall into the atmosphere,

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.100
causing the atmosphere to glow
and creating beautiful auroras.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.400
As with any sort of weather—
most of the time, things are fine.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.340
Sometimes, there are hurricanes, though.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.580
Or in the case of the Sun,
solar superstorms.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.320
And we know that they happen
once or twice every century.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.740
If one were to happen today, we
would first detect strong solar flares,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.840
a sort of flash before the much
more dangerous thunder.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.640
The thunder is a CME, consisting of
billions of tons of hot magnetic plasma

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.800
that crosses the 150 million kilometers
between the Sun and Earth

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.040
in less than a day.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:46.140
When it arrives, it causes a shockwave that
violently compresses the Earth's magnetic field

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.720
and transfers energy into
the magnetosphere.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.519
But it can get worse.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.800
If the magnetic field of the CME is
aligned to Earth's in just the right way,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.800
the two magnetic fields merge.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:02.220
As the magnetic cloud passes over Earth,
it stretches the Earth's field into a long tail.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:06.600
Eventually, the energy stored in the
tail becomes too much to contain.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.760
It snaps and explosively releases
its energy towards Earth.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.040
A geomagnetic storm has begun.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.720
A few hundred years ago,
nobody would have cared.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.620
This storm gushing over the Earth is not relevant
for machines made out of meat and bones.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.800
But it's very relevant for machines
made out of metal and wire.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.640
Remember the dynamo?

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.480
Magnetism creates electric currents.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:37.240
Earth in the 21st century is covered in millions
of kilometers of wires, transporting electricity,

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.480
and a complex grid of machines, like
transformers, that make this transfer possible.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:47.360
A CME's energy can induce currents in our power
grid that can either completely shut it down,

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.640
or worse, destroy the transformer
stations that keep our grid running.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.800
This has happened already,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.300
like when the Quebec power grid failed
after a strong solar storm in 1989.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.360
But in general, our engineers know
how to deal with these storms,

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.580
and so we usually don't even notice.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.580
The last time a solar hurricane
washed over Earth was in 1859:

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.140
The Carrington Event,

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.580
the largest geomagnetic storms
ever observed on Earth.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.560
Massive auroras occurred
as far south as the Caribbean.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.240
In some places, they were
so bright that people got up,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.840
thinking the Sun was rising.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.440
Luckily, we only had one
sort of modern technology:

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:28.740
telegraph systems.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.080
They failed all over the world,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.880
shocking their operators
and chucking out sparks.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.940
Today, we have a tad more technology,

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.140
and our luck may run out soon.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.660
Another bad solar storm is
bound to happen eventually.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.180
A storm as strong as the Carrington Event
missed earth only by a small margin in 2012.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:53.500
Studies projected that it would've inflicted
serious damage to electronic systems globally,

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:57.540
costing up to $2.6 trillion to the US alone.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.180
The time to replace all the damaged systems
was estimated at between 4 and 10 years.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.660
It's hard to say how bad
it could have been.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.420
Experts disagreed.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.880
Some assumed there would
just be temporary blackouts,

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.080
but others worried it
could be much worse.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.840
We won't know for sure until
a big solar hurricane hits us.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.680
The probability of such an event is
estimated to be 12% per decade.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:25.160
That's about a 50/50 chance of
at least one in the next 50 years.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.300
And, there is more unsettling news.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:32.500
A 2019 paper found that
even calm stars, like our Sun,

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.860
can create superflares
every few thousand years.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.680
Eruptions orders of magnitude
stronger than the strongest storms

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.680
we have observed in the Solar System.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.680
If such a storm hits us unprepared, the
consequences could be catastrophic.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.600
It's hard to overstate how much
we depend on electricity.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.580
It's not just the lights at home.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.100
It means no computers,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.400
no communication,

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:56.720
no navigation.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.200
A sustained power outage might lead
to a breakdown of the supply chain,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.020
water supply systems failing

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.560
and hospital generators running dry,

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.900
supermarkets not being refilled

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.000
while food rots in the fields.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:14.500
The lack of power might make it extremely
hard to reboot our broken power grid,

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:18.920
taking years or decades to
restart our starving civilization.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.280
Okay, time to panic?

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:26.100
As much as daily newspapers might like for
solar storms to send us back to the Stone Age,

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.360
they probably won't.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.700
Fortunately, even though solar
storms aren't preventable,

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.440
virtually all of their nasty side effects are.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:39.340
Scientists observing the Sun have a few hours
up to a few days to see a CME coming.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.920
And the engineers working the
systems that keep the world running

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:46.120
are well aware of the risks
posed by solar storms.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.100
Transformers and substations
can be taken offline—

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.000
short preventative blackouts—

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.420
or in other words, by unplugging stuff.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:58.300
Engineers can open up extra lines
to dissipate the extra power.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:03.500
And with investment and upgrades cheap
compared to those other natural disasters require,

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.820
we could protect the world's electric grid
against even the nastiest of storms.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.240
But we do need to prepare.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.040
While the risk is manageable, it is real.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:16.960
For while our Sun bathes us
in warm and pleasant light,

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.960
one day, it might send a monster our way

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.820
that we better be ready for.

